WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.850
Hi! In continuous writing, you get a topic and three pictures, and you write a story. Today let's learn how to choose a picture and plan a great story.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.018
Say the topic is A Kind Act, and the three pictures show a wallet, rain and an old lady.

3
00:00:18.168 --> 00:00:30.306
You usually need to use at least one picture, so read the instructions carefully. Choose the one you have most to say about that fits the topic, like finding a wallet and returning it.

4
00:00:30.456 --> 00:00:39.906
Spend a few minutes planning before you write. A story has four parts. The opening can hook the reader with speech, a sound or a question.

5
00:00:40.056 --> 00:00:44.994
The build-up is where the problem appears: you find a wallet full of money.

6
00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:50.082
The climax is the most exciting part: will you find the owner in time?

7
00:00:50.232 --> 00:00:57.210
The ending gives your reflection, such as how helping someone made you feel and what you learnt.

8
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.562
The story has already happened, so write it in the past tense, all the way through.

9
00:01:02.712 --> 00:01:08.634
Instead of just I was scared, try My knees shook and my mouth went dry, so the reader can feel it.

10
00:01:08.784 --> 00:01:15.594
Add details you saw, heard, smelt and felt, like the rain drummed on the roof.

11
00:01:15.744 --> 00:01:22.242
Watch out! Some pupils try to use all three pictures, and the story turns into a muddle.

12
00:01:22.392 --> 00:01:29.154
One picture is usually enough. What matters is that the story fits the topic and is well developed.

13
00:01:29.304 --> 00:01:35.034
When you finish, check: is my story really about a kind act?

14
00:01:35.184 --> 00:01:48.786
Your turn! Which opening is better? A: One day, I went to school. B: Whose wallet is this? I gasped. Pause and think.

15
00:01:48.936 --> 00:01:56.874
The answer is B. It starts with speech and hooks the reader straight away. Did you get it right?

16
00:01:57.024 --> 00:02:06.858
Another one! Make I was very happy more vivid by showing, not telling. Pause and think.

17
00:02:07.008 --> 00:02:16.410
You could write: A big smile spread across my face and I could not stop grinning. Your answer may be different, and that's fine.

18
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:30.522
Here's another! Which ending is better? A: Then I went home. B: I learnt that a small act of kindness can make a big difference. Pause and think.

19
00:02:30.672 --> 00:02:38.466
The answer is B. It gives a reflection and links back to the topic. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:51.114
One more! Which sentence shows rather than tells? A: I was tired. B: My legs felt like jelly. Pause and think.

21
00:02:51.264 --> 00:02:58.554
The answer is B. The reader can feel how tired you were. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:58.704 --> 00:03:13.698
Remember: choose the best picture and plan before you write. Give your story an opening, build-up, climax and ending. Use the past tense, show feelings, add sensory details, and end with a reflection that fits the topic.

23
00:03:13.848 --> 00:03:18.594
Try writing a story opening with speech today!

